# Gate review — Python C3 slice M4 (integer module v4 float text kernels + portable float renderer)

**Verdict: APPROVE-WITH-NITS.**

Read-only review of the uncommitted state (`git diff HEAD -- src docs test` plus the three new files). No files edited, no suites run; every claim below was checked against the diff, the fixture text, and the existing module code.

## 1. Attack-list verification

**(1) float-digits — correct.** The floor/ceiling bracketing in `int-float-digits` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc`, new "Float text (version 4)" section) is sound: a rounding interval is convex and contains `|v|·10^k`, so any n-digit decimal outside `[floor, ceil]` in the interval would force floor or ceil in too — checking both is sufficient, and nearest-only is genuinely insufficient below powers of two (asymmetric interval), exactly as designed. The `hi?` guard when `r = 0` is safe (an exact `lo` always reads back). Tie-break `2r vs den`, half to even on `lo`'s parity, is right (hi = lo+1 flips parity). The `10^n` ceiling bump (`exp10 = e + (count text) − n`) is correct, and a trailing-zero first-survivor is impossible (the same real number would have survived at a shorter n), so post-strip exponents are right. `float-ratio` is exact for subnormals (×2^64 below 2^11 keeps intermediates < 2^53; powers of two always representable). `decimal-exponent` corrects the bit-length estimate exactly and terminates. Pinned: `5e-324 → ["5" -324]`, max double `→ ["17976931348623157" 308]`, and both power-of-two asymmetry cases (`0060000000000000`, `4580000000000000`), whose fixture rows I confirmed (float-text-v1.txt:262-264, 3578-3580). Zero/NaN/±inf → `:wrong-type` `:finite-nonzero-float`, and non-float arg 0, all pinned. No host float parse/print anywhere on the path (`pow2`, host big ops, `host/to-radix-string` only). See nit 3 on the 17-digit cap.

**(2) decimal->float — correct.** One exact `ratio->float` per input; the sticky tail is never re-rounded. Truncation keeps 800 + sticky 1; since trailing zeros are stripped first, the dropped tail always contains the final nonzero digit, so an unconditional sticky 1 is exact. The thresholds agree with the exact path at both boundaries: s = 310 implies value ≥ 10^309 > max double, so both paths give `+inf` (and `ratio->float`'s `::float-overflow` keyword → `##Inf`, the only keyword it can return — underflow returns `0.0`, not a keyword); s = −326 implies value < 10^−325 < 2^-1075·(midpoint), so both paths give `0.0`. After truncation `e ∈ [−1127, 309]`, so `host/to-native` and `pow10` (table + squaring, exercised up to ~10^1075 by the padded-midpoint rows) are safe. Never a limit reason: no `out` call, no `::max-digits` read — proven by the 5001-digit fast pin (`1` + 5000 zeros at −5000 → 1.0) and the `0e99999999999999999999` fixture row (→ +0.0, so the all-zeros path short-circuits before the exponent is used). Malformed refusals pinned: empty / non-digit (`"1.5"`, `"-1"`) → `:syntax`; non-string digits, non-integer exponent → `:wrong-type`. The `9007199254740993.000000000000000001` fixture row (→ `4340000000000001`) is a precise double-rounding discriminator — a to-float-then-scale mutant answers `4340000000000000`.

**(3) max-digits — correct.** `['max-digits [0] int-max-digits]` answers `(::max-digits limits)`; `integer-profiles` derives `:pure` from the same exports table (`src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc:898`), so the three new exports are profiled automatically; arity refusal pinned in `module-version-4-test`.

**(4) v3/S0 unchanged, no host traps.** The `int-parse` refactor into `digit-values!`/`digits->big` preserves semantics and check order (digit-limit check before digit validation, `refuse!` 'parse :syntax identical). `fast-kernels` untouched; new exports take the default (non-fast) path. No other namespace pins version 3 (grep over test/ and src/). New top-level defs (`small-powers-of-ten`, `two-64-float`, `two-minus-11-float`) are pure computed values — Dart-lazy is fine; the CLJD load-side-effect trap doesn't apply. No JS BigInt, no JVM `bigint`-of-double, no `parseDouble`/`toString` on any kernel path; `#?(:cljd ...)` first in all three new conditional sites.

**(5) render float-repr — CPython-exact.** `digits-repr` branch-checks against CPython's rule: fixed iff −4 ≤ e < 16 (integer part via digit splice, `.0` when `n ≤ e+1`, `0.` + zeros for negative e), else `d[.ddd]` + `exponent-text` with ≥2 digits (`e+16`… `e+308`, `e-05`). Cond order NaN → ±inf → zero(−0.0/0.0) → digits matches CPython. **Flips: none — independently confirmed.** I grepped the whole test tree for exponent-form float text and for rendered decimal pins: the only hits are numeric literals in unrelated namespaces (postgraphics/cbor), comments, and Python *source inputs*; the renderer-adjacent pins (`float_address_test:104` "2.0 3.0 2.0 -0.0 0.5 1", `prelude_parity_test:1512-1534`) are all inside the fixed range and unchanged. The engineer's "no flips" claim holds.

**(6) Tests — honest and discriminating.** Generator asserts 3.9.6, has an explicit ops list, runs from repo root, and *measures* every expected (`repr(x)`, `float(s)` in try/except); floats are `f:` bit patterns, strings are hex code points, error messages asserted ASCII and tab-free. Committed beside the fixture per the int-contract-v1 pattern. Loader extends `int_ops_fixtures/parse`; the int-ops branch is the old code unchanged (v1-v3 byte-identical); blank lines removed before magic (Dart trailing blank safe); `float-text-row` validates 4/5-field shapes. Batches `partition-all 400` (23 repr + 2 float_str); big test `^:slow` under `slow/guard`. No `0.0`/`1.0` literals in portable tests (values from `fx/bits->double`/`cbor/float64-from-bits`; `-1.0` only as the sanctioned negate); `#?(:cljd ...)` first; no doseq destructuring over >32 elements (doseq is over ≤23 *batches*; row loops use `keep`/`into`). Discriminating rows verified present for every claimed mutation: nearest-only (binade rows above), host str (all 9,174 repr rows), both notation thresholds (`1e16`, `9999999999999998.0`, `0.0001`, `1e-05` rows + pins), sticky (1,068-digit operands, max operand 3,224 chars), double rounding (`%.30e` rows + the 4340000000000001 row). Coverage matches design §6's list item by item (binades −1074..1023 with neighbors, powers of ten ±323..308, thresholds, halfway Decimals at 13 anchors, overflow edge 2^1024−2^970 both sides, 1e400/1e-400, signed zero, underscores, the full malformed list). Could a wrong implementation pass? Not one that errs on any of the seven mutated axes; the remaining unpinned surface is deliberate (nit 4).

**(7) Deviations and questions** — ruled below.

## 2. Findings

1. **Comment states 767; the true bound is 768.** `src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc`, `max-significant-digits` docstring ("a double's midpoint has at most 767 significant digits"); same figure repeated in the engineer report. The midpoint between the smallest normal and the largest subnormal, (2^53−1)·2^−1075 = (2^53−1)·5^1075·10^−1075, has **768** significant decimal digits (log10 = 15.9546 + 751.3934 = 767.35; odd×odd, no trailing zeros); so does (2^53+1)·2^−1075. The architect's design §1 has the same off-by-one. **No soundness impact**: 800 kept + sticky = 801 > 768 with margin. Severity: nit (doc), but it is the justification for a load-bearing constant — someone shrinking 800 toward the stated bound would create a real bug. Exact fix: change the docstring to "at most 768 significant digits" (and optionally note the design figure was off by one, per the fixture-wins/errata convention).
2. **`yin.vm.integer-test` carries no v4 semantics.** Version 3 has `float-fixture-test`, `float-named-cases-test`, `float-refusals-test` (`test/yin/vm/integer_test.cljc:501-599`); version 4 adds only the version pin and export-set names, leaving all v4 behavior in the yang tree beside the fixture. Severity: nit. Exact fix: add one small `float-text-named-cases-test` (~10 lines) — the two power-of-two asymmetry pins, one sticky-truncation pin, one `:finite-nonzero-float` refusal pin, and the `max-digits` pin — mirroring `float-named-cases-test` style.
3. **No explicit 17-digit cap in the search loop.** `int-float-digits` recurs `(inc n)` unbounded; design §1 says "for n = 1 to 17". Behaviorally identical (17 significant digits always round-trip a binary64, so the loop provably stops ≤ 17), but the invariant is only implicit while the report describes a bounded loop. Severity: nano-nit; fix optional (a `:when`/assert at 18, or a comment).
4. **`:special` float_str rows are bits-unchecked in M4.** `float_text_test.cljc:319` skips inf/nan splits, so the fixture's `inf`/`-inf`/`nan`/`-nan` bit expectations (e.g. line 9868, `-nan` → `fff8000000000000`) bind nothing yet. Correct for M4's scope — the kernel has no word-form path — but it must not be inherited silently. Severity: nit (forwarding note). Exact fix: S4's guest `float()` test must check bits on those rows; see Q2.

## 3. Rulings on deviations

- **Tests under `test/yang/python/antlr/` instead of `test/yin/vm/`:** approved. The brief itself put the loader extension in `int_ops_fixtures` (yang tree), and the fixture is a CPython artifact, not a module-only one. Compensate with finding 2's small addition so the module's own convention test covers its version bump.
- **`host-names` not updated:** approved, and required — the brief forbids any prelude edit in M4, and `host-names` lives in `prelude.cljc`. The module docstring documents the exports; S4's brief already lists the three names.
- **Extra refusals** (`:syntax` for non-digit text; `:wrong-type` for non-string digits / non-integer exponent; JS integral Numbers accepted as floats): approved. Each follows an existing module convention (`parse`'s `:syntax`, `big!`'s `:wrong-type`, and `host-float?`'s JS rule already shared by `compare-float`/`to-float`), and they replace undefined behavior the design left unstated.

## 4. Rulings on the open questions

- **Q1 — should `integer_test` get v4 pins: yes** (finding 2). The v3 convention is exactly that, and version 4 otherwise leaves the module's own namespace blind to its bump.
- **Q2 — `float('-nan')` sign bit: S4 must match the bits** (`fff8000000000000`, fixture line 9868). Repr hides the sign, but content identity, dict keys, and any later signbit/copysign observe it, and the bit pattern is host-observable today. Build the sign by pinned bit manipulation (or a pinned host op), never `(* -1.0 nan)`, whose sign bit is host-dependent.
- **Q3 — `int(str)` whitespace: measure it in `int-conv-v1`; do not share `float`'s set by assumption.** The float measurement already proves CPython 3.9.6 does not strip by `str.isspace` for `float` (28–31 refused while `isspace` is true), so the two builtins demonstrably differ in at least one direction; `int`'s set must be measured before S4 hard-codes anything. Also fold disagreement 2 into S4's message work: whitespace-only strings quote `''`, everything else quotes the unstripped original.

## 5. Other notes (no action)

- The engineer's three fixture disagreements are each backed by rows I verified in the fixture (9850, 9856-9859, 9868); the architect's §2 "exactly `str.isspace`" is wrong for `float`, fixture wins.
- Generator dedups rows via `dict.fromkeys` — harmless; counts (9,174 / 749 / 62) match both the report and my greps.
- Line lengths: the only two >80-column lines in the touched files are pre-existing and untouched (`integer.cljc:542`, `render.cljc:104`, both present at HEAD). New code is ASCII.
- Kondo/cljstyle and the three-lane runs are taken from the engineer report; this review ran nothing (read-only), but the discriminating rows for every claimed red mutation were verified present in the fixture.

Landing this with nits 1 and 2 addressed (both one-comment/one-small-test sized) is my recommendation; nits 3-4 can ride into S4's brief.

